Title: Armed Conflict and Acute Food Insecurity: Evidence from a High-Frequency District-Level Panel
Abstract: This paper examines the causal effect of armed conflict on acute food insecurity using a harmonized district-by-quarter panel linking ACLED conflict data and FEWS NET food security classifications. The dataset covers 1,159 districts across 22 developing and fragile countries from 2010 to 2025. Using an instrumental variable based on the spatial diffusion of conflict, the paper finds that conflict raises the probability of food crisis by 5-7 percentage points in the affected quarter. The long-run effect is roughly twice as large as the contemporaneous impact. Three key transmission channels are identified: food prices, agricultural production, and forced displacement. The findings imply that emergency food aid alone is insufficient; policy must simultaneously stabilize markets, support agricultural recovery, and address the needs of displaced populations.
Keywords: armed conflict, food insecurity, IPC, ACLED, FEWS NET, fragile states, Africa, displacement, food prices
